const staticAudit = () => agent(
  `${WHERE}
APK FILES — write source-app/static.md: what the source app's own files show. Base APK: "${BASE}"${src.apks.length > 1 ? `; splits: ${src.apks.slice(1).join(', ')}` : ''}. Tools (all installed): A = ${AAPT}, AA = ${APKANALYZER}. Put every raw output in ${SCRATCH}/static/ and read it with grep; nothing raw goes into the project.
1. \`A dump badging <base>\` → package, versionName, versionCode, minSdk, targetSdk, application-label, launchable-activity, every uses-permission and uses-feature, native-code, supports-screens, the number of locales.
2. \`AA manifest print <base>\` → every activity, service, receiver and provider; every intent-filter with its action, scheme, host and mimeType (deep links; share targets: android.intent.action.SEND); every meta-data NAME (com.google.android.gms.ads.APPLICATION_ID = AdMob, com.facebook.sdk.ApplicationId, com.google.firebase.*, com.onesignal.*).
3. Framework, from \`unzip -l <base>\`: lib/*/libflutter.so → Flutter; assets/index.android.bundle → React Native; lib/*/libunity.so → Unity; assets/public/ or assets/www/ → Capacitor / Cordova; only classes*.dex → native Kotlin/Java.
4. Code. Flutter: L = lib/arm64-v8a/libapp.so (else armeabi-v7a, x86_64); \`unzip -p <base> L | strings -n 6 > ${SCRATCH}/static/libapp.txt\`; the app's own Dart package = \`grep -oE 'package:[a-z0-9_]+/main\\.dart' ${SCRATCH}/static/libapp.txt\`; its own files = \`grep -oE 'package:<it>/[A-Za-z0-9_/]+\\.dart' … | sort -u\` (every screen, model, service and widget file of the app — its structure); the packages it uses = \`grep -oE 'package:[a-z0-9_]+/' … | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\`. No package: paths (an obfuscated build) → record "Dart symbols obfuscated — structure NOT OBSERVED". Native: \`AA dex packages --defined-only <base>\` → the top-level packages (SDKs) and the app's own package's classes (C lines): its Activities, Fragments, ViewModels and services. If \`command -v jadx\` finds jadx: \`jadx -d ${SCRATCH}/jadx --no-res <base>\` and read the app's own package for its formulas, validation rules and limits (cite file:line); without jadx write "jadx not installed — rules come from the walk".
5. Texts: \`A dump resources <base> | grep -A1 " string/"\` → the default-locale UI texts and error messages (skip the abc_*, mtrl_*, common_google_* library strings); Flutter: the asset list \`unzip -l <base> | grep flutter_assets/\` (fonts from FontManifest.json, images by name, l10n .arb/.json files — read those for the texts).
6. Network and backend: URL hosts in the code (\`grep -oE 'https?://[A-Za-z0-9.-]+' ${SCRATCH}/static/libapp.txt | sort -u\`, or the same over \`unzip -p <base> "classes*.dex" | strings\`), and the backend resources (google_app_id, project_id, firebase_database_url, gcm_defaultSenderId — "Firebase project present", never the values).
7. Colours: \`A dump resources <base> | grep -A1 " color/"\` and the theme parents in the manifest's application theme (DayNight → dark mode); a Flutter app keeps its colours in code — write "Flutter: colours are not in the resources" then.
${STATIC_EXTRA}
Write source-app/static.md:
# APK — <package> <versionName> (<framework>)
## Facts — one line each, with the command it came from
## Dependencies and capabilities — one line per item: \`- D01 · <SDK, Dart package, permission, deep link, share target, service or receiver> — <the capability it gives this app> (<the exact evidence line>)\`. Every permission, every SDK and Dart package that is not a framework internal (flutter, material_color_utilities, collection, meta, vector_math, …), every intent-filter and every own service or receiver gets an id.
## App's own code — one line per own screen file or Activity/Fragment: \`- D<nn> · screen: <path or class>\`; the models and services as plain lines
## Layouts — the L lines of step 8 (native)
## Data in the code — the data lines of step 9 (their D ids continue the numbering)
## Texts — the UI texts, grouped by screen or prefix; the error messages under their own heading
## Network and backend — hosts, Firebase services, keys present (names only)
## Colours — name → #RRGGBB, or the Flutter line
\`${P} log source "APK: <framework>, <n> dependencies, <n> own screens, <n> layouts, <n> data tables"\`. Return the counts.`,
  { label: 'audit: APK files', phase: 'Audit', schema: obj(['framework', 'dependencies', 'ownScreens', 'layouts', 'dataTables'], { framework: str, dependencies: int, ownScreens: int, layouts: int, dataTables: int, obfuscated: bool }), effort: 'high' },
)
// The walk drives a device, so it runs as the plugin's tester sub-agent (agents/tester.md), like every device step of build-app.
const PKG = src.androidPackage || '<package>'
// Steps 1, 2 and 8 of the walk per device (the user's answer to "walk", or to "walkfallback" for a second walk)
const deviceFor = (mode) => ({
  emulator: {
    what: "the plugin's own Android emulator af-standard (installed from the APK set)",
    start: `\`${P} devices claim "${DIR}"\` — if it fails, another project's run holds the devices: return walked 0 with that message as note and do nothing else. Then \`${P} devices up android --sdk "${ANDROID_SDK}"\` → its serial (S below), and \`${P} maestro-install\`.
2. Install: \`${INSTALL.replace('<serial>', 'S')}\` (no -g: the app's own permission requests are part of what you record). Install fails → record the error, run step 8, return walked 0 with it as note. The app opens on Play's licence screen (pairip) → record it with a screenshot, run step 8, return walked 0 with "stopped at Play's licence screen" as note.`,
    end: `\`${ADB} -s S uninstall ${PKG}\`, \`${ADB} -s S emu kill\`, \`${P} devices release "${DIR}"\`.`,
  },
  play: {
    what: "the plugin's own Android emulator af-standard, with the app installed from Google Play by the user",
    start: `\`${P} devices claim "${DIR}"\` — if it fails, another project's run holds the devices: return walked 0 with that message as note and do nothing else. Then \`${P} devices up android --sdk "${ANDROID_SDK}"\` → its serial (S below), and \`${P} maestro-install\`.
2. Install from Play: \`${ADB} -s S shell am start -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d "market://details?id=${PKG}"\`, then \`${P} log source "waiting for you: in the emulator window sign in to Google Play and tap Install for ${PKG} (up to 30 minutes)"\`. Check every 30 s with \`${ADB} -s S shell pm path ${PKG}\`, in foreground commands of at most 9 minutes each (\`for i in $(seq 18); do ${ADB} -s S shell pm path ${PKG} | grep -q package: && break; sleep 30; done\`), up to 30 minutes in all. Not installed by then → run step 8, return walked 0 with "not installed from Play within 30 minutes" as note.`,
    end: `\`${ADB} -s S emu kill\` (the app and the Google account stay on that emulator; never uninstall or sign out for the user), \`${P} devices release "${DIR}"\`.`,
  },
  phone: {
    what: `the user's phone ${src.phoneModel || src.phone} (serial ${src.phone}), where the app is installed — the user chose it`,
    start: `S = ${src.phone}. \`${P} phone save S\` (its settings, put back at step 8), and \`${P} maestro-install\`.
2. No install: the app is on the phone. Never install, uninstall, clear data or change anything outside the app; the app keeps what the user had. The first launch and the fresh-install empty states cannot be seen here — write "NOT OBSERVED (walked on the user's installed app)" for them in step 5.`,
    end: `\`${P} phone restore S\` (airplane mode, dark mode and the other settings back as they were). Leave the app installed.`,
  },
}[mode] || null)
const walk = (mode) => () => {
  const WALK_ON_DEVICE = deviceFor(mode)
  const prompt = `${WHERE}
WALK — every screen of the source app on ${WALK_ON_DEVICE.what}, written to source-app/walk.md with a screenshot of each. Use no other device.
1. ${WALK_ON_DEVICE.start} Launch: \`${ADB} -s S shell monkey -p ${PKG} -c android.intent.category.LAUNCHER 1\`, then \`${P} wait-app S --platform android --app ${PKG} --sdk "${ANDROID_SDK}"\` (never a fixed pause).
3. Walk breadth-first from the first screen, by the element list only (the android-screen tool \`read_screen\` and adb at its at= points — never coordinates read off a screenshot). A screen is new when its title or its set of controls differs from every screen recorded (the same screen with other values is not new). For each new screen:
   - \`read_screen S\`, then \`${ADB} -s S exec-out screencap -p > source-app/screens/S<nn>-<slug>.png\` (on macOS then \`sips --resampleWidth 540 <file>\`);
   - in walk.md: \`## S<nn> · <its title>\`, then "screenshot: source-app/screens/S<nn>-<slug>.png", "reached: S<nn> → tap \\"<label>\\"", "elements:" every element top to bottom (kind — text, button, icon button, field, toggle, checkbox, slider, tab, list item, image —, its exact text, label or hint, its value), and "actions:" each control → what it did (opens S<nn>, a dialog or sheet with its exact texts, a toast with its text, a system screen, nothing);
   - tap every control once and come back (Back, or the screen's own back control; confirm with read_screen). Scroll every scrollable area to its end and record what appears. In a list open the first item only. Every tab, menu, drawer and overflow item counts as a control.
   Never buy (at the store's purchase sheet record the plans and prices shown, then Back), never sign in with a real account or create one (record the sign-in options, then stop on that branch), never send, post, call, share to a person, rate, report or delete an account (record the sheet or intent, then Back). An ad: record its type (full screen, banner, native), the screen and the tap that brought it, then close it.
4. Every input field: submit it empty, then one invalid value for each rule it may have (too long, letters in a number, negative, zero, a malformed email or URL), then a valid value; record "- field S<nn>/<label>: <input> → \\"<the exact message>\\" (<where it shows>)". Every calculation or generated output (a total, a conversion, a score, a generated text or file): run it with 3 different inputs and record "input → output" exactly, so the formula can be derived from them.
5. States: the first launch (onboarding, the permission dialogs: which permission, on which screen, its text — then allow it); empty states (this is a fresh install); offline — \`${ADB} -s S shell cmd connectivity airplane-mode enable\`, reopen each screen that loaded data and record what it shows, then \`… disable\`; persistence — create one record and change one setting, \`${ADB} -s S shell am force-stop <package>\`, relaunch, record what is still there; dark mode — \`${ADB} -s S shell cmd uimode night yes\`, reopen S01, one screenshot source-app/screens/S01-dark.png, record whether the app follows it, then \`night no\`; notifications it posts (\`${ADB} -s S shell dumpsys notification --noredact | grep -A4 <package>\`).
6. End of walk.md: "## Navigation" — a table | from | tap | to | with one row per action of step 3; "## Fields and outputs" — the lines of step 4; "## States" — step 5; "## Not reached" — every control that leads somewhere you did not go, and why.
7. \`${P} log source "walk: <n> screens, <n> fields, <n> outputs"\` after every 5 screens and at the end.
8. Always, also after a failure: ${WALK_ON_DEVICE.end}
Return walked (screens), fields, outputs, navigation (rows), notReached, note.`
  const opts = { label: mode === WALK_MODE ? 'audit: walk' : `audit: walk (${mode})`, phase: 'Audit', schema: obj(['walked', 'fields', 'outputs', 'navigation', 'notReached'], { walked: int, fields: int, outputs: int, navigation: int, notReached: strs, note: str }), effort: 'high' }
  return agent(prompt, { ...opts, agentType: 'app-factory:tester' }).catch((e) => {
    log(`walk: tester sub-agent unavailable (${String(e && e.message || e).slice(0, 120)}) — running as a plain agent`)
    return agent(prompt, opts)
  })
}
// Evidence an earlier run wrote is kept (HAVE: its ids); only the missing parts run. A walk with no screen runs again.
const kept = (name, n, unit) => async () => { log(`audit: ${name} kept from the earlier run (${n} ${unit})`); return { kept: true } }
const [st, sa, wk] = await parallel([
  HAVE.store ? kept('store.md', HAVE.store, 'F ids') : store,
  ...(APK ? [HAVE.static ? (NEED_STATIC ? staticSupplement : kept('static.md', HAVE.static, 'D ids')) : staticAudit] : [async () => null]),
  ...(HAVE.walk ? [kept('walk.md', HAVE.walk, 'screens')] : CAN_WALK ? [walk(WALK_MODE)] : []),
])
const shown = (r, f) => (!r ? 'no result' : r.kept ? 'kept' : f(r))
log(`audit: store ${shown(st, (r) => `${r.features} feature(s), ${r.screenshots} screenshot(s), ${r.pages} page(s)`)}${APK ? ` · APK ${shown(sa, (r) => r.framework ? `${r.framework}, ${r.dependencies} dependencies, ${r.ownScreens} own screens, ${r.layouts} layouts, ${r.dataTables} data tables` : `kept, added ${r.layouts} layouts and ${r.dataTables} data tables`)}` : ''} · walk ${HAVE.walk || CAN_WALK ? shown(wk, (r) => `${r.walked} screen(s), ${r.fields} field(s), ${r.outputs} output(s)${r.note ? ` — ${r.note}` : ''}`) : 'not run'}`)
if (!st) return { aborted: true, stop: 'the store audit returned no result — run again' }
// The walk ran and reached no screen (or returned nothing): the second place the user chose at the start, in this run —
// no question now. No second place, or it fails too → no walk; the Report says why in Sources.
let wk2 = null
if (CAN_WALK && !HAVE.walk && !(wk && wk.walked > 0) && FALLBACK !== 'none' && canWalkOn(FALLBACK)) {
  log(`walk: no screen on ${WALK_MODE} (${((wk && wk.note) || 'no result').slice(0, 120)}) — walking on ${FALLBACK}, as answered at the start`)
  wk2 = await walk(FALLBACK)()
}
const WALKED = Boolean(HAVE.walk || (wk && wk.walked > 0) || (wk2 && wk2.walked > 0))
const STATIC = APK || HAVE.static > 0 // static.md exists: from this run or an earlier one
if (CAN_WALK && !WALKED) log(`walk: no screen reached${wk2 ? ` on ${WALK_MODE} or ${FALLBACK}` : ''} — PRD.md comes from the files and screenshots; nothing is asked`)

// Gaps → decisions, not questions: every behaviour PRD.md needs that the evidence does not show gets the option closest
// to the evidence and the answers, recorded (source-app/answers.md, DECISIONS.md) — the run never stops after the start.
phase('Decide')
const gaps = await agent(
  `${WHERE}
GAPS → DECISIONS — nothing is asked after the start of this run: you decide each gap and record it. Read the evidence in full, in one turn: source-app/store.md${STATIC ? ', source-app/static.md' : ''}${WALKED ? ', source-app/walk.md' : ''}, source-app/answers.md (when it exists; its "## Decided without asking" section holds the gaps an earlier run decided — keep them, do not decide them again). PRD.md (written next) must state for the new app: platforms (iOS, Android, tablets, minimum OS, orientation, languages, dark mode); every screen's elements, actions and empty / loading / error / offline states; every field's type, required, limits and exact error messages; every feature's inputs → rules → outputs (formulas, limits, what it stores); data and backend (what is stored where, accounts, sync, backup; each external service a feature needs); monetisation (ads, plans and prices, what Premium unlocks, free limits); permissions and when each is asked; notifications; settings; and for the new look: font, corner radius, elevation, icon style. For each of these the evidence and the answers do not show exactly (no quoted text, number or behaviour with its id), one decision: id gap-<n> (numbered after the highest gap id in answers.md), a question naming the screen or feature and what is unknown (ending with "?"), 2 to 4 options (each a label of at most 5 words and a one-line description of exactly what gets built), the FIRST one = the one closest to the evidence and the answers, why = which evidence lacks it and why the first option is closest. One unknown per decision.${CAN_WALK && !WALKED ? ` One of them is the walk: it reached no screen (${[WALK_MODE, ...(wk2 ? [FALLBACK] : [])].join(' then ')}) — question "The walk reached no screen on the devices tried: how is PRD.md written?", first option "${NO_WALK}" (screens from the layouts and the store screenshots), why = what each walk returned.` : ''}
Record them: (1) append to source-app/answers.md a section "## Decided without asking" (once) and per decision "### gap-<n> · <question>", "Decided: <first option's label> — <its description>", "Why: <why>"; (2) write them as a JSON list to a file in your scratchpad — each {id, area: "scope", header: <at most 12 characters>, question, options: [{label, description}] with " (Recommended)" at the end of the first label, affects: <screen or feature>, ask: false, why} — and run \`${P} decisions --questions <that file> --add\`, then a second file [{id, answer: <the first label>, isDefault: true, by: "decided"}, …] and \`${P} decisions --answers <that file>\` (DECISIONS.md lists each as decided without asking). \`${P} log source "gaps: <n> decided without asking"\`. Return the number decided and their ids.`,
  { label: 'gaps → decisions', phase: 'Decide', schema: obj(['decided'], { decided: int, ids: strs }), effort: 'high' },
)
log(`gaps: ${gaps ? `${gaps.decided} decided without asking (source-app/answers.md, DECISIONS.md)` : 'no result'}`)

// ───────────────────────────── Report: PRD.md + the source app's theme, gated by the checker ─────────────────────────────
phase('Report')
const report = await agent(
  `${WHERE}
REPORT — write PRD.md and source-app/theme.json from the evidence: source-app/store.md${STATIC ? ', source-app/static.md' : ''}${WALKED ? ', source-app/walk.md' : ''} , source-app/answers.md when it exists (the user\'s answers; nothing under its "## Superseded" is used or cited)${HAVE.static || APK ? ', source-app/assets.md (the app\'s own files, A ids)' : ''} and the screenshots they list (Read the ones you need). Read every evidence file in full first, in one turn. PRD.md is what /app-factory:build-app builds from: the new app has EVERY capability of the source app, reached the same way (same screens, controls, order and flows), with its OWN theme, icon, images, animations and sounds (the user's app) — and nothing it did not have. ${WALKED ? '' : STATIC ? 'The walk did not run (see walk.md or its absence): screens come from the APK\'s own screen files and the store screenshots — say so in Sources. ' : 'There was no APK: screens come from the store screenshots (P ids) and the listing only — say so in Sources; a rule no source states is an answer from the start or a recorded decision (source-app/answers.md). '}Never change source-app/*.md.${HAVE.prd ? ` PRD.md from an earlier run is here (${HAVE.prdProblems} check problem(s)): continue from it — run the check first, fix what it lists, and change only the lines the evidence written in this run adds to or corrects; never write it again from the start.` : ''}
Every requirement line — each bullet, numbered line and table row outside "Theme", "Reused from the source app" and "Sources" — ends with its source: [walk: S07], [walk: S07 → S09], [store: F03], [store: P02], [store: <url>], [static: D04], [static: L12], [web: <url>], [user] (an answer in source-app/answers.md, or the user's note) or [decided: gap-<n>] (a gap decided without asking, recorded in answers.md's "## Decided without asking"). A text in quotes or a name in backticks is copied from the evidence exactly (only the source's brand replaced by the new name). Nothing is asked and nothing is guessed: a behaviour no evidence, answer or recorded decision gives is decided now the way the Decide step did it — the option closest to the evidence and the answers, the next gap id, recorded in answers.md and DECISIONS.md (\`decisions --questions <file> --add\` and \`--answers\` with by "decided") — and cited [decided: gap-<n>]. "ASSUMED" or "NOT OBSERVED" may appear only on a line that also cites the [user] answer or the [decided: gap-<n>] that settled it. A URL you cite must be in store.md's "Fetched" list.
Sections — exactly these headings, numbered, in this order:
## 1. App — the FIRST line exactly "- Name: **<the new name>** [user]" with the new name the user answered at the start: ${ARGS.name ? JSON.stringify(String(ARGS.name)) : ANS.name ? JSON.stringify(ANS.name.replace(/^Keep /, '')) : 'the name the user answered'}; what it does, one sentence; the source app (title, developer, package, App Store id, version audited); then "- Package: <id>" — the Android applicationId, which is also the namespace and MainActivity's package (the team's apps have them equal): the source's package when the app ships as an update of it, as the answers say — and "- iOS bundle id: <id>": the source App Store app's bundleId (\`curl -s "https://itunes.apple.com/lookup?id=<iosId>"\` → bundleId) when it has one, else the Android package.
## 2. Platforms — iOS and Android phones; tablets only if the source supports them (supportedDevices, supports-screens); minimum OS; orientation; languages; dark mode as the source — each from the evidence or the user's answer.
## 3. Screens — one "### S<nn> · <name>" per screen (${WALKED ? 'each S id of walk.md' : 'each own screen file or store screenshot'}): purpose; every element top to bottom with its exact label or hint (functional texts kept as they are; the source's brand name replaced by the new name); each action → where it leads; its empty, loading, error and offline states.
## 4. Navigation — the | from | tap | to | table${WALKED ? ' (every row of walk.md\'s)' : ''} and the first-launch flow.
## 5. Fields — | screen | field | type | required | limits | exact error messages | placeholder |, one row per input field; a limit no source shows: its recorded decision, [decided: gap-<n>].
## 6. Features — one "### <capability>" per capability: inputs → rules → outputs (a formula derived from the walk's input → output records, with the records quoted), what it stores, its errors and limits.
## 7. Data and backend — what the source keeps on the device and what on its servers (Data safety, the walk's persistence check, the SDKs); accounts and sign-in options; sync; backup. For the new app: the same capability on its OWN services — never the source's servers, endpoints, Firebase project or keys. Each external service a feature needs (sign-in, a cloud database, an AI or other paid API, maps) is one line "<feature> needs <service> — no key provided", so /app-factory:build-app's contract finds a free no-key service or a "not available" message for it, or asks for the key.
## 8. Monetisation — ads seen (type, screen, the tap that showed it), the plans and prices, what Premium unlocks (the paywall's texts), free limits seen. (build-app adds the team's ads + Premium pattern; this tells its contract what the source did.)
## 9. Permissions and notifications — each permission: when it is asked (screen) and what it is for; each notification posted.
## 10. Settings — every setting and what it changes.
## 11. Theme — the source app's own look, kept (it is the user's app). Measure its palette with \`${CHECK} palette <screenshot.png>…\` (the 12 most used exact colours) on ${WALKED ? 'S01 and two other walk screenshots' : 'the store screenshots (convert a jpeg or webp first: `sips -s format png <f> --out ' + SCRATCH + '/<name>.png`)'}${STATIC ? ', and static.md\'s Colours (the APK\'s colorPrimary and named colours are exact: they win over a measured pixel)' : ''}; primary = the colour of its app bar or main buttons, secondary = its accent; say where each was read. Then the table | role | light hex | dark hex | used for | with the ten roles primary, onPrimary, secondary, onSecondary, background, onBackground, surface, onSurface, error, onError: primary and secondary ARE the source's (theme.json source = colors, checked); every other role is the source's colour for that use where the evidence shows one, else chosen so text is at least 4.5:1 on its base. Dark hexes only if the source follows dark mode (walk S01-dark, or a DayNight theme). Then the rest of the look, as the source has it: its font (static.md res/font — reused from source-app/assets/font), corner radius, elevation and icon style as the screenshots and layouts show them. Never ask for, offer or pick a different colour, font or icon style.
## 12. Reused from the source app — one row per A id of source-app/assets.md: | A id | file | where the new app uses it (launcher icon, an image slot by name, a Lottie animation on its screen, a sound on its event, the font) |, or "not used — <why>" (a library file: an ad network's close or sound icon). Every reusable file the app's screens need is used, never redrawn or replaced by a Material icon. Then "Images not in the APK": each image slot the screens show whose file is not in source-app/assets/ (in split APKs that were not given) — | slot | subject | Openverse search words | — the build fetches each with \`${CHECK} image\`. The app's own servers and endpoints the evidence shows are used as they are (they are the user's); a key found in the APK is never written in a file: it is stored with \`${P} secret set <NAME>\` and named here. The launcher icon is the source's (A id) — Flutter's default only when the APK has none.
## 13. Coverage — | id | what was seen | where it is in this PRD | built or left out (why) |, one row for EVERY S, F, D, P and A id in the evidence files. Leave out only an SDK with no user-visible capability (analytics, crash reporting, attribution, the framework's own) or a file "Reused" marks not used — every other id is built.
## 14. Sources — every URL fetched, the APK (file name, package, version, \`shasum -a 256\`)${STATIC ? '' : ' — "no APK was given or found on a connected phone"'}, the walk (device, date)${WALKED ? '' : ' — "not walked: <why>" (the devices tried and what each returned)'}, every answer of source-app/answers.md (id, question, answer), every decision made without asking (gap id, question, decided option, why), and what could not be observed.
The screenshots in source-app/ show the source app (whose theme is kept): they are evidence, never mockups or design — PRD.md never calls them that (build-app would compare the new app's pixels with them).
source-app/theme.json: {"source": {"primary": "#RRGGBB", "secondary": "#RRGGBB", "evidence": "<where each was read>"}, "colors": [{"role": "primary", "hex": "#RRGGBB", "use": "<what>"}, … all ten roles], "dark": [the same ten, only with dark mode]} — the exact hexes of the Theme table, colors.primary and colors.secondary equal to source.primary and source.secondary; build-app's colour check then lets the code use these colours and no others.
Then run \`${CHECK} check .${STATIC ? ' --apk' : ''}${STATIC && !WALKED ? ' --no-walk' : ''}\`. It lists every problem: a guess without the user's answer, a quoted text or backticked name that is not in the evidence, an untagged line, a vague word, an id from the evidence missing in PRD.md, a cited id or URL the evidence does not have, a file named that does not exist, a missing section, a theme role, a primary or secondary that is not the source's, a file of assets.md not placed, or text below 4.5:1. Fix PRD.md or theme.json and run it again until it prints "audit ready" — never weaken a line to pass (a requirement turned into a vague one fails review the same way).
Then \`rm -rf "${SCRATCH}"\` (the raw dumps; source-app/assets/ and assets.md stay — the build reuses them) and \`${P} log source "PRD.md ready: <n> screens, <n> features, <n> fields, <n> ASSUMED, <n> NOT OBSERVED"\`. Return the counts and the checker's last line.`,
  { label: 'report: PRD + theme', phase: 'Report', schema: obj(['ready', 'screens', 'features', 'fields', 'assumed', 'notObserved', 'check'], { ready: bool, name: str, screens: int, features: int, fields: int, assumed: int, notObserved: int, check: str }), effort: 'high' },
)
if (!report || !report.ready) {
  log(`PRD.md did not pass the audit check${report ? `: ${report.check}` : ''} — fix what \`source-app.mjs check .${STATIC ? ' --apk' : ''}${STATIC && !WALKED ? ' --no-walk' : ''}\` lists, then run /app-factory:app-dev-without-prd again in this folder (it continues from here)`)
  return { aborted: true, report }
}
log(`PRD.md ready${report.name ? ` for "${report.name}"` : ''}: ${report.screens} screen(s), ${report.features} feature(s), ${report.fields} field(s), ${report.assumed} ASSUMED, ${report.notObserved} NOT OBSERVED`)
const buildArgs = `{"noFigma":true${NOTE}}`
return {
  done: true,
  report,
  evidence: { store: st, apk: sa || null, walk: wk || null, apkFrom: src.apkFrom },
  nextStep: `Tell the user in two lines: the source app was audited (${report.screens} screens, ${report.features} features, ${report.fields} fields; ${report.assumed} ASSUMED, ${report.notObserved} NOT OBSERVED — listed in PRD.md), and PRD.md, source-app/ (evidence and screenshots) and source-app/theme.json (the new palette) are in the project. Then, in this same chat, run the workflow app-factory:build-app (the /app-factory:build-app command) with args ${buildArgs} and follow its nextStep as always, without waiting for the user in between: it ${HAVE.contract ? `re-audits the CONTRACT.md already here against the updated PRD.md (changed, added and removed criteria; the code in lib/ is kept and continued, not rebuilt)` : 'writes the contract from PRD.md'} without asking anything (every contract gap is decided and recorded in DECISIONS.md — the questions were all asked at the start), then develops the app, adds the ads once development passes its gate, runs the device flow tests on the app with ads, then senior QA and the final audit — this is the same one command the user started.`,
}
